  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0151\" consists of 14 tables. The primary purpose of this schema is to manage various aspects of an asset management system.\nThe \"announcements\" table is used to store announcements made by users. It has columns for the announcement id, the user id, the body of the announcement, the action text and url, and timestamps for creation and update. The user id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"users\" table.\nThe \"assets_departments\" table is a junction table that links assets and departments. It has two columns for asset id and department id, both of which are primary keys. The asset id and department id are foreign keys referencing the id in the \"assets\" and \"departments\" tables respectively.\nThe \"password_resets\" table is used to store password reset requests. It has columns for the email, token, and timestamps for creation.\nThe \"categories\" table is used to store categories of assets. It has columns for the category id, name, parent category id, and timestamps for creation and update. The parent category id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"categories\" table.\nThe \"invoices\" table is used to store invoices. It has columns for the invoice id, user id, amount, due date, status, and timestamps for creation and update. The user id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"users\" table.\nThe \"assets\" table is used to store assets. It has columns for the asset id, name, serial number, manufacturer id, category id, location id, department id, user id, and timestamps for creation and update. The department id, manufacturer id, user id, and category id are foreign keys referencing the id in the \"departments\", \"manufacturers\", \"users\", and \"categories\" tables respectively.\nThe \"api_tokens\" table is used to store API tokens. It has columns for the token id, user id, name, token, expiry date, and timestamps for creation and update. The user id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"users\" table.\nThe \"notifications\" table is used to store notifications. It has columns for the notification id, user id, message, read status, and timestamps for creation and update. The user id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"users\" table.\nThe \"tickets\" table is used to store support tickets. It has columns for the ticket id, asset id, user id, status, description, and timestamps for creation and update. The asset id and user id are foreign keys referencing the id in the \"assets\" and \"users\" tables respectively.\nThe \"maintenance\" table is used to store maintenance records. It has columns for the maintenance id, asset id, description, date, and timestamps for creation and update. The asset id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"assets\" table.\nThe \"departments\" table is used to store departments. It has columns for the department id, name, location id, and timestamps for creation and update. The location id is a foreign key referencing the id in the \"locations\" table.\nThe \"locations\" table is used to store locations. It has columns for the location id, name, address, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"manufacturers\" table is used to store manufacturers. It has columns for the manufacturer id, name, and timestamps for creation and update.\nFinally, the \"users\" table is used to store users. It has columns for the user id, email, name, password, remember token, and timestamps for creation and update. The email is unique to prevent duplicate users.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an open-source IT asset management system called Snipe-IT. This system is designed to assist organizations in efficiently tracking and visualizing various assets including hardware, software licenses, accessories, and consumables. Built on the Laravel framework, Snipe-IT provides a user-friendly web interface that facilitates asset management tasks.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objectives of Snipe-IT are to enable organizations to:\n- Track and manage IT assets effectively.\n- Visualize asset histories and statuses.\n- Implement efficient asset check-in and check-out processes.\n- Generate and utilize barcodes for asset tracking.\n- Send automated email notifications for asset-related updates.\n- Manage user access through role-based controls.\n- Integrate with LDAP/Active Directory for streamlined user management.\n- Utilize an API for automation and enhanced integration.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\nThe main stakeholders and users of Snipe-IT include:\n- IT teams in businesses, schools, and non-profits.\n- System administrators responsible for asset tracking and management.\n- End-users who interact with and check out assets.\n- Organizational decision-makers interested in analytics and reporting.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Asset Management: \n   - Track hardware, software licenses, accessories, and consumables.\n   - Maintain detailed asset histories including maintenance and ticket records.\n   - Perform asset check-in and check-out operations.\n\n2. Barcode Generation: \n   - Generate barcodes for assets to facilitate physical tracking.\n   \n3. Notifications: \n   - Send automated email alerts for asset updates, upcoming maintenance, and more.\n\n4. Role-Based Access Control: \n   - Implement permissions and access controls based on user roles.\n   \n5. LDAP/Active Directory Integration: \n   - Align user management with organizational directories.\n   \n6. API Access: \n   - Provide API endpoints for external integration and automation.\n\n7. Multi-User and Multi-Location Support: \n   - Enable system use across various user profiles and geographical locations.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Usability: \n   - Ensure a user-friendly web interface that is easy to navigate.\n\n2. Scalability: \n   - Support multiple users and locations without degradation of performance.\n\n3. Flexibility: \n   - Allow for customization and self-hosting to meet diverse organizational needs.\n\n4. Security: \n   - Ensure secure data handling, access control, and integration processes.\n\n5. Reliability: \n   - Maintain consistent performance and availability across installations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0151.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0151\nCREATE TABLE `announcements` (\n  `announcement_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `body` text NOT NULL,\n  `action_text` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `action_url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`announcement_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `announcements_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assets` (\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `serial_number` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `manufacturer_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `location_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`asset_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`manufacturer_id`) REFERENCES `manufacturers` (`manufacturer_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`category_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `departments` (`department_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assets_departments` (\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`asset_id`, `department_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_departments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`asset_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_departments_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `departments` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `api_tokens` (\n  `token_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expiry_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`token_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `api_tokens_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `categories` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `parent_category_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `categories_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`parent_category_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`category_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `departments` (\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`department_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `departments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`location_id`) REFERENCES `locations` (`location_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invoices` (\n  `invoice_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `due_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`invoice_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoices_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `locations` (\n  `location_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`location_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `manufacturers` (\n  `manufacturer_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`manufacturer_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenance` (\n  `maintenance_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`maintenance_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`asset_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `notification_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `message` text NOT NULL,\n  `read_status` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`notification_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `notifications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tickets` (\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ticket_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `tickets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`asset_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `tickets_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
